# The composed remainder, worked out two ways. One row per composed layer, with the figure worked
# out from its parts beside the one worked out from its totals, and whether they agree.
#
# The disagreements are the conversions with a range
#   The layers that disagree are exactly those whose parts convert through a factor that is a
#   range, and `spread` says which those are. Every other composed layer converts at one or at a
#   single value, so there is nothing to take in twice and the figures agree exactly; those are
#   the controls, and without them a difference here would show nothing.
#   algebra/composed_remainder holds both halves on every run.
#
# Disagreement does not mean a document is wrong
#   layers/differenced_remainder.sqlc sets the composed totals against each other, and one
#   conversion factor multiplied both of them, so its figure takes that factor's range in twice.
#   The figure from the parts converts each part's own remainder, so the factor counts once. Both
#   are correct arithmetic; only the one from the parts is the remainder, and layers/remainder.sqlc
#   carries it. This section is a referral, not a violation.
-- composition/fused_remainders.sqlc, with the two figures set against each other.
SELECT f.composition                                             AS "composition!",
       f.composed_layer                                          AS "composed_layer!",
       f.parts                                                   AS "parts!",
       f.unit                                                    AS "unit!",
       f.pivoted_low::float8                                     AS "pivoted_low!",
       f.pivoted_mode::float8                                    AS "pivoted_mode!",
       f.pivoted_high::float8                                    AS "pivoted_high!",
       f.derived_low::float8                                     AS "derived_low!",
       f.derived_mode::float8                                    AS "derived_mode!",
       f.derived_high::float8                                    AS "derived_high!",
       f.spread                                                  AS "spread!",
       (f.pivoted_low = f.derived_low
    AND f.pivoted_mode = f.derived_mode
    AND f.pivoted_high = f.derived_high)                         AS "agrees!"
FROM (
    :compose(composition/fused_remainders.sqlc)
) f
ORDER BY "agrees!", f.composition, f.composed_layer
